"confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "Under the required 600-unit relay constraint, 1385's time-respecting forward set contains just 2 nodes and does not include 1186 (step_01). Relaxing the constraint entirely still fails to reach 1186 despite a 340-node forward set (step_02), showing the obstruction is event ordering rather than insufficient slack. Both endpoints are minimally active (one event each, step_03); 1385 only emits to hub 639 (step_05) and 1186 only receives from 1818 (step_06, step_07), so 1186's single inbound event is the sole possible entry point. 1385 does reach 1818 via 639 and 1479, but only with hop waits of 1066 and 1205 (both >600) and arriving too late to feed the 1818->1186 event (step_08). The dense static neighbourhood around hub 639 (step_04) is therefore a time-ignoring artifact, not a cascade."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion: no cascade.**\n\nKey findings:\n- **step_01**: With the required \u0394\u2264600 constraint, 1385 reaches only 2 nodes; 1186 is *not* reached.\n- **step_02**: Even with *no* waiting-time limit, 1385's temporal forward set spans 340 nodes but still excludes 1186 \u2014 the failure is structural/ordering, not just a slack-time issue.\n- **step_03\u201306**: Both endpoints are active in the window but extremely thin (1 event each). 1385 only sends to hub 639; 1186's sole contact is an *inbound* edge from 1818 (step_07), so 1186 never forwards anything and can only be reached via 1818.\n- **step_08 (confounder control)**: 1385 *can* temporally reach 1818 (route 1385\u2192639\u21921479\u21921818, arriving t=76747), but hop waits are 1066 and 1205 \u2014 both exceed 600 \u2014 and the 1818\u21921186 event does not occur after that arrival (else step_02 would have reported 1186 reached). So the apparent static connectivity via the 639 hub (step_04) is a time-ignoring artifact.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8980b60f\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1385, 1186, 639, 1479, 1818],\n  \"window\": [61549, 99948],\n  \"confidence\": 0.91,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Under the required 600-unit relay constraint, 1385's time-respecting forward set contains just 2 nodes and does not include 1186 (step_01). Relaxing the constraint entirely still fails to reach 1186 despite a 340-node forward set (step_02), showing the obstruction is event ordering rather than insufficient slack. Both endpoints are minimally active (one event each, step_03); 1385 only emits to hub 639 (step_05) and 1186 only receives from 1818 (step_06, step_07), so 1186's single inbound event is the sole possible entry point. 1385 does reach 1818 via 639 and 1479, but only with hop waits of 1066 and 1205 (both >600) and arriving too late to feed the 1818->1186 event (step_08). The dense static neighbourhood around hub 639 (step_04) is therefore a time-ignoring artifact, not a cascade.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 30627, "total_output_tokens": 2557, "total_tokens": 33184, "total_latency_ms": 39685, "llm_calls": 5, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}